<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Prome Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1482 CE – 1542 CE · capital Prome</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Burmese kingdom based at Prome on the lower Irrawaddy that broke from Ava in 1482 and fell to the rising Toungoo in 1542.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1482 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thado-minsaw-of-prome">Thado Minsaw</a>, the governor of Prome and a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narapati-i-of-ava">Narapati</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ava-kingdom">Ava Kingdom</a>, rebelled during a succession crisis and made his town, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy</a> near the old Pyu city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-ksetra-kingdom">Sri Ksetra</a>, the seat of an independent kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Prome controlled the river trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> coast and played Ava, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hanthawaddy-kingdom">Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a> and Toungoo against each other. Its kings at times allied with the Shan rulers who took Ava in 1527, receiving support from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/confederation-of-shan-states">Confederation of Shan States</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> besieged Prome after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, and in 1542 the city fell despite help from the Shan confederation and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mrauk-u-kingdom">Arakan</a>. Prome was absorbed into the Toungoo realm and later served as a provincial centre; it is the modern city of Pyay.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1482 CE – 1542 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.19%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1482 CE – 1542 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Prome</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thado Minsaw, Narapati of Prome</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Burmese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Prome, Pyay</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1512&amp;focus=prome-kingdom" aria-label="Prome Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7257 1480 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prome Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7577 1736L7574 1626M7577 1736L7609 1732"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7574 1626h0M7609 1732h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7577 1736h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7577 1736h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1512&amp;focus=prome-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
